# Machine-readable citation metadata. GitHub reads this file and offers "Cite this
# repository"; Zenodo and several reference managers read it too.
#
# The R package has no file of its own: `citation("platypus")` is generated from its
# DESCRIPTION, which is R's own convention, and altdoc renders that into the Citation page
# on its site. This is the same facts by the convention of this language, and a test holds
# its `version` to pyproject.toml and `pyplatypus.__version__` so it cannot quietly go stale.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "pyplatypus: computer vision for medical imaging"
abstract: >-
  Semantic segmentation and object detection for medical images, specified as one
  configuration file or a few lines of code. The engine behind the platypus R package.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Maj
    given-names: Michal
version: 0.8.0a9
# The year a citation carries. Read from here rather than from the clock: a page built next
# year must not silently restate the citation as next year's.
date-released: "2026-10-08"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/maju116/pyplatypus"
url: "https://maju116.github.io/pyplatypus/"
keywords:
  - computer-vision
  - medical-imaging
  - segmentation
  - object-detection
  - pytorch
